WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Anyone who wants to understand&nbsp;
Vladimir Putin needs to know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.680
story of what happened to him one&nbsp;
night in East Germany 33 years ago.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.640
It was December 5, 1989, just a few&nbsp;
weeks after the Berlin Wall fell.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.120
In East Germany, communism was dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.560
although free democratic elections&nbsp;
weren’t scheduled until the spring.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.040
In the east German city of Dresden, protestors&nbsp;
stormed the regional headquarters of the East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.360
German secret police, the Stasi,&nbsp;
completely overrunning the office.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.160
Then, a small group of protestors - reportedly&nbsp;
no more than 20 - decided to head across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.800
street to the local headquarters of&nbsp;
the Soviet secret service, the KGB.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.120
Russian media later mythologized the incident and&nbsp;
exaggerated the number of demonstrators to 5,000

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.560
Putin anticipated the protestors’&nbsp;
arrival and called for help from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.840
Soviet military stationed in East&nbsp;
Germany to protect the employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.680
in the office as well as the files&nbsp;
they had on informants and agents.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:55.440
They tried to burn as many of the files&nbsp;
as possible and even broke the furnace!

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.560
But the Red Army was not coming to their rescue.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.560
In his biography, First Person, Putin&nbsp;
recalled being told on the phone:

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.360
"We cannot do anything without orders&nbsp;
from Moscow. And Moscow is silent.”

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.520
Those words, “Moscow is silent”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.040
stayed with him because it finally dawned on him&nbsp;
that the Soviet Union had all but disappeared.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.160
Now it was up to Putin to figure&nbsp;
out how to get himself and his KGB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.560
colleagues out of that dangerous mess.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.440
How could he stop the crowd&nbsp;
from swarming the building?

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.280
In an interview with the BBC, one of&nbsp;
the protestors, Siegfried Dannath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.000
recalled how when the mob approached the KGB&nbsp;
house, the guard immediately rushed inside.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.320
Shortly afterward, “an officer&nbsp;
emerged - quite small, agitated”.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.280
That officer was 37-year-old KGB&nbsp;
Lieutenant Colonel Vladimir Putin.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.600
Except he didn’t say he was a spy.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.520
Putin said he was a translator.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.760
He told the crowd in fluent German that&nbsp;
the property was on Soviet territory.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.560
According to the protestor, Putin&nbsp;
told the group to leave, quote:

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.240
“Don’t try to force your way into&nbsp;
this property. My comrades are armed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.520
and they’re authorized to use&nbsp;
their weapons in an emergency.”

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.800
Putin was bluffing.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.200
But the mob believed him and backed away.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.960
Leaving staff and the sensitive files unscathed. 
&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.680
Long before Vladimir Putin became president&nbsp;
of Russia, he fulfilled his teenage wish of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.320
becoming a KGB agent - inspired by the&nbsp;
books he read and films he watched.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.800
He spent five years stationed in the isolated&nbsp;
East German city of Dresden, from 1985 to 1989.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.040
East Germany was important to Moscow. It&nbsp;
was a satellite state of the Soviet Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.560
that sat on the doorstep of NATO,&nbsp;
the western military alliance.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.600
We don’t have much information&nbsp;
about what kind of work Putin did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.720
in Dresden. He’s said little&nbsp;
about his work in the eighties.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.720
Other than speaking about&nbsp;
that December day in 1989.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.880
What he witnessed was humiliating.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.920
He saw the west winning the Cold War as&nbsp;
the Eastern bloc crumbled under his feet.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.320
The Atlantic wrote, “Putin's experience&nbsp;
in Dresden undoubtedly reinforced his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.840
view of his identity and role as the critical&nbsp;
outsider learning from the mistakes of others.”

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.400
Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev failed to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.160
the subsequent collapse of&nbsp;
the Soviet Union in 1991.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.480
One of the largest and most powerful empires in&nbsp;
history unraveled, breaking up into 15 nations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.520
leaving Russia a shadow of its former self.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.560
Putin called it “the greatest geopolitical&nbsp;
catastrophe of the twentieth century.”

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.760
He later claimed he knew&nbsp;
the collapse was inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.200
“I understood that a position based on walls and&nbsp;
water barriers cannot exist forever. But I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.560
something different to rise in its place. And&nbsp;
nothing different was proposed. That's what hurt."

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.640
Putin never rose to the top ranks of&nbsp;
the KGB, he remained a mid-level agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.920
on the periphery. In the nineties, he left the&nbsp;
KGB to become deputy mayor of St. Petersburg, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.640
by the late nineties, he transferred to Moscow to&nbsp;
work in President Boris Yeltsin's administration.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.640
He was picked to lead the new Federal&nbsp;
Security Service which replaced the KGB.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.360
And then he was surprisingly chosen&nbsp;
by Yeltsin as prime minister in 1999.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.080
He saw how weak Russia had become.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.280
When President Bill Clinton approved airstrikes&nbsp;
against Serbian forces in Bosnia - Washington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.560
didn’t even consult Yeltsin. The Serbs share&nbsp;
deep ethnic and religious ties with Russia.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.080
Putin’s leadership would be very different from&nbsp;
Yeltsin’s when he took over as President in 2000.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.600
He impressed Clinton’s point man on Russia,&nbsp;
Strobe Talbott, the US Deputy Secretary of State,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.640
who wrote in his memoirs that he&nbsp;
was struck by Putin’s “ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.520
convey self-control and confidence&nbsp;
in a low-key, soft-spoken manner”.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.320
Putin’s image though is anything but low-key.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.360
He’s frequently photographed&nbsp;
doing super manly things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.836
like fishing shirtless, overpowering&nbsp;
opponents in judo, or driving race cars.

00:04:40.268 --> 00:04:44.880
The macho President has put his tough image on&nbsp;
display for both his fellow countrymen and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.480
world - reinforcing the image of a strong leader&nbsp;
trying to build up a strong, resurgent country.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.040
In 1999, just before taking office, Putin&nbsp;
stressed the importance of strengthening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.480
the state, writing in an article in the&nbsp;
Russian newspaper Nezavisimaya Gazeta:

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.200
“Russia is in the midst of one of the most&nbsp;
difficult periods in its history. For the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.640
time in the past 200-300 years, it is facing&nbsp;
a real threat of sliding to the second, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:16.560
possibly even third, echelon of world states. We&nbsp;
are running out of time for removing this threat.”

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.320
Many believe his actions over the years&nbsp;
have been guided by one principle:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.560
to “make Russia great again”

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.640
When Russia annexed the Crimean Peninsula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.120
from Ukraine in 2014 - it led to a&nbsp;
spike in Putin’s popularity at home.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.720
And late on February 23rd, 2022, Russia&nbsp;
started attacking targets all over Ukraine.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.160
Why? The clearest answer came from Putin himself.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.840
Two days prior to the attack, Putin&nbsp;
delivered a speech where he described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.800
Ukraine as an illegitimate country, that&nbsp;
it sits on “historically…Russian land”.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.000
He views Ukraine as a lost part of Russia itself.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.000
But Ukraine doesn’t see it that way.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.280
It voted overwhelmingly to&nbsp;
establish an independent state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.880
and now seeks to join NATO which&nbsp;
Putin is determined to block.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.160
Putin’s closest aides today consist of those from&nbsp;
his KGB days or who have security backgrounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.280
They are believed to tell him&nbsp;
only what he wants to hear.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.160
He Putin is said to want to rebuild a sphere&nbsp;
of influence in Eastern Europe, bemoaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.440
the collapse of the communist order which&nbsp;
traumatized him as a young agent in East Germany.&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.560
In this latest attack on Ukraine, a Russian&nbsp;
tank could be seen flying the Soviet flag.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.840
Officially, Russia has dismissed the&nbsp;
possibility of reviving the Soviet Union.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.800
Putting this story together from start to finish&nbsp;
in just a couple of days was not easy to pull off.

